Close the findings that can be closed, and record why the others stand

Defender for Cloud raised four things against the hosted service and the
workload backend. Two are now fixed, one is a development-only advisory
deliberately deferred, and one is accurate about the mechanism but not
about the risk.

The App Service accepted plain HTTP and now does not. Verified before
changing anything that nothing legitimate calls it that way - the
frontend, the notebooks and Fabric all use HTTPS, and the only http://
in the repository is a localhost callback. After the change /health
still answers 200 over TLS, HTTP returns 301, and the notebook snapshot
path still returns its pinned sequence.

The backend's test runner is past the esbuild advisory. That upgrade
crosses two major versions, so it was made and then verified rather than
assumed: 39 tests pass and npm audit reports nothing. Vitest 4 also
widened its default include pattern and began running every suite twice,
once from whatever JavaScript happened to be left in dist/, which would
have reported passes for code that no longer exists. A config now scopes
it to src/.

The frontend's build tool is not upgraded. The same advisory applies,
but the fix is a three-major jump in Vite that changes the emitted
bundle, and the surface it would ship to is a live workload that cannot
be functionally verified outside Fabric. It builds cleanly and the
change is reversible, so this is a judgement rather than an obstacle:
shipping an untested build to close a development-server advisory is the
wrong trade.

API Management authenticates nothing - no subscription requirement, no
policy on either API. That is accurate, and it is not an open door.
Probed with no credential, the management API and every instance data
route answer 401; only the version endpoint is public, by design. The
finding is about defence in depth, and the honest improvement -
validate-jwt on the management API, which cannot extend to the instance
plane because instance tokens are not JWTs - is recorded as not yet
done rather than quietly skipped.

